
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: what counts as a "change" worth a notification. Getting this wrong is the single most common way notification
  systems become something people mute.

  Two rules I'd bake in regardless of your answer, so flagging rather than asking:

  - [1mNever notify you about your own action.[0m You know what you just did. Every mature system does this and it's cheap at
    event time.
  - [1mCollapse rapid repeat edits to the same task.[0m Someone fixing a typo three times shouldn't generate three bells. A
    short coalescing window on the unread record handles it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how wide the notifiable-event set is. Narrow means high signal and some genuine misses; wide means nothing
  is missed and the bell stops meaning anything. This is cheap to tune later [3mif[0m every mutation emits an event and the
  filtering happens at notification-creation time — the expensive version is deciding at mutation time which changes are
  "worth" emitting, because unemitted history can't be recovered.

  So: emit everything, filter on the way out. Then the question is only which events pass the filter in v1.

  My recommendation: the first four below. Status, assignment, due date, and comments are the changes that alter what
  someone has to [3mdo[0m. Title and description edits usually don't, and they're the highest-volume events — they're the ones
  that would drown the rest.

